  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n**********Rounding Out Today's storms into Tonight*************\n\nOngoing storms across the region will continue through this evening. \nA few spotty lines will likely move through, right now about two \nwaves are visible but model data is pointing toward a third forming \naround sunset in isolated pockets that still contain reasonable \ninstability. With these showers, widespread minimum rainfall totals \nwill be .5\" for the CWA north of the parkways; isolated values in \nthe .45\" have already fallen and rain is expected to continue. \nAdditionally, local values in the 2-4\" range are still possible \ngiven the moisture-loaded soupy environment. Training is a possible \nthreat if storms slow down around sunset, but at the moment things \nare moving along nicely, just dumping rain. Flash flooding may be \npossible in known flash-ier spots, but river/stream flooding is \nunlikely. \n\nOvernight, we are unlikely to dry out. Lows in the upper 60s and \ndews at the same means we will see fog, potentially dense fog, in \nareas that received rainfall but are not actively raining. This will \ncertainly include river valleys and low-lying areas, and will likely \npersist beneath cloud cover.\n\n****************Tomorrow's Heat and S IN Storms******************\n\nAs the sun rises tomorrow, we aren't likely to see it as cloud cover \nwill persist throughout the day. However, that doesn't mean we get \nrid of the heat - expect around the 90 degree mark in Bowling Green \nand low 80s across the northern half of the CWA. We should start \nclearing around noon in patches, with widespread clearing around \nsunset. On the off-chance that Southern IN does see earlier \nclearing, severe storms are possbile in the evening hours. The wind \nprofile is ideal for low-level directional shear and upper-level \nspeed shear, with a hodograph favorable for a low-end tornadic \nthreat. While this is a tornadic environment, there are too-many \n\"if\"s involved in this statement to portray any kind of confidence \nof occurence; this is outlooked in the Day 2 SPC forecast with the \nmajority of the threat pushing toward central IN and OH. \nThundershowers across S IN have a timeframe of about 2pm-10pm.\n\n\n*******************3-Day Weekend Forecast************************\n\nFriday we see a cold front move through the area in the afternoon. \nOur high temps will come just after sunrise, with highs in the low \n60s (S IN) to 70 degree mark (Bowling Green, Campbellsville, \nRichmond) and a 75 degree high toward Albany, KY. We are breezy all \nday, with sustained speeds in the 10-15 kt range and gusts up to 25 \nkt. Cloud cover and some spotty showers arrive with our front, and \novernight lows are expected to be in the 30s. \n\nSaturday and Sunday are firmly in the pleasant fall weather \nterritory, with highs in the 55-65 range and overnight lows around \n40 on Saturday night and pushing frost territory in the mid-30s \nSunday night. Clear skies should remain all Saturday and Sunday.\n\nNext week stays dry, and we get to keep the seasonable temperatures \nfor a while longer. Our next chance of rain should arrive around the \n24th.",
